#!/bin/sh
# script/ci-build: the entry point CI uses to run the tests on a clean machine.
#
# Unlike script/test, it provisions dependencies first, because a CI runner starts bare. This is
# what the workflow's test job runs, so CI exercises the same bootstrap a developer does. The
# lint and format checks are separate CI jobs (script/lint, script/fmt) so they fail fast and in
# parallel. See notes/scripts.md and .github/workflows/ci.yml.
#
# Name: ratified 2026-07-22 by adopting Scripts to Rule Them All. The canonical set keeps its
# standard names even where a more descriptive word exists, because the entire value is that the
# command is the same in every repository that follows the pattern (notes/naming.md). Respelled from
# `cibuild` on 2026-08-01 (milestone 63) under the rule that shell entry points are hyphenated,
# because an underscore or a squash in a command name reads as a mistake next to `apt-get` and
# `pkg-config`.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

script/bootstrap
cargo xtask test

# **The interactive boot, in the same job rather than a job of its own** (milestone 230). Until that
# milestone `script/shell-check` ran in neither `script/test` nor CI, so it was red on `main` on both
# architectures for five days and a fully green tree said nothing about it: init trapped before the
# console existed, and the only gate that boots the real init is this one.
#
# **It earned its place on its first run here.** The aarch64 leg passed and the riscv64 leg failed,
# on a check whose local runs were green on both, and the divergence was real rather than flaky
# infrastructure: the kernel's fault printer and the userspace console server drive the same UART
# from two address spaces with nothing arbitrating, so a thread dying while init prints shuffles the
# two lines together at byte granularity. That happens on every boot; whether it lands on top of a
# line the gate reads is timing, and a runner is not this laptop. Local-only would have kept it
# invisible.
#
# Here, and not as a thirteenth workflow job, because of what the two cost. Measured on 2026-09-02:
# the check itself is **30 seconds** for both architectures against a warm target directory, while
# this job's build is around ten minutes. A separate job would pay that ten minutes again, plus its
# own QEMU cache restore and runner slot, to do thirty seconds of work; appended here it reuses the
# build that just ran and adds only the `--features shell` kernels. Runner concurrency is a real
# ceiling on this project (CLAUDE.md's lane-count section), so a job that buys nothing the step
# cannot is a job that slows every other lane's group build down.
#
# After `cargo xtask test` rather than before it: the suite is the broader signal and should be what
# fails first when both would.
cargo xtask shell-check
